OpenJornada M5Stack firmware third-party notices

Scope
-----

This inventory applies to `.pio/build/m5stack_release/firmware.bin`. It does
not apply to the OpenJornada container image. Distribute this file and the
complete `LICENSES/` directory with every firmware binary.

The pre-existing `LICENSES/DejaVu-Fonts.txt` applies only to the bitmap subset
generated from DejaVu Sans and embedded in
`include/openjornada/assets/spanish_font.hpp`; it is not a license for the
firmware or for the libraries below.

Linked components
-----------------

- Arduino-ESP32 2.0.16, packaged by PlatformIO as
  `framework-arduinoespressif32` 3.20016.0: LGPL-2.1-or-later, with individual
  framework files also carrying their own Apache-2.0 or MIT notices. Its
  precompiled SDK is based on ESP-IDF 4.4.7, whose project license is
  Apache-2.0 and whose bundled components retain file-level notices. See
  `LICENSES/Arduino-ESP32-LGPL-2.1.md` and `LICENSES/Apache-2.0.txt`.
- ArduinoJson 7.4.2: MIT. See `LICENSES/ArduinoJson-MIT.txt`.
- M5GFX 0.2.11 and M5Unified 0.2.8: MIT. See
  `LICENSES/M5Stack-MIT.txt`.
- MFRC522_I2C commit `8152dddc93cf743397ac225e34bf268698326664`:
  the pinned source contains a public-domain declaration and author history but
  no separate license file. The exact declaration is preserved in
  `LICENSES/MFRC522-I2C-PUBLIC-DOMAIN.txt`; no different license is inferred.
- GCC runtime and Newlib material linked by the PlatformIO Xtensa toolchain:
  see `LICENSES/GCC-GPL-3.0.txt`,
  `LICENSES/GCC-RUNTIME-LIBRARY-EXCEPTION-3.1.txt` and
  `LICENSES/Newlib-NOTICES.txt`.
- The TLS certificate bundle embedded by Arduino-ESP32 is described upstream
  as root certificates obtained from Mozilla. Certificates and third-party
  data retain the rights and notices of their issuers; this inventory does not
  infer a software license for that data.

PlatformIO itself, esptool and filesystem-image utilities are build tools and
are not part of `firmware.bin`.

Source and relinking obligations
--------------------------------

License notices alone do not satisfy source-code, object-file, relinking or
written-offer obligations that may apply when distributing a statically linked
firmware binary. A distributor must review and provide the corresponding
materials required by the component licenses for the exact firmware build.
